 Simple Calculator in C

Today, I created a Simple Calculator using the C programming language.

Through this program, I learned:

How to use the double data type to store decimal numbers.
How to declare and initialize variables for numbers, operators, and results.
How to use scanf() to get numbers and operators from the user.
How to use %lf to read double values and %c to read a character.
How to use the switch statement to perform different arithmetic operations.
How to use arithmetic operators such as +, -, *, and /.
How to use if-else inside a switch case to check division by zero.
How to use the default case to handle invalid operators.
Important Logic / Formula

Arithmetic Operations

Addition: result = num1 + num2
Subtraction: result = num1 - num2
Multiplication: result = num1 * num2
Division: result = num1 / num2

Important Variables

operator – Stores the arithmetic operator.
num1 – Stores the first number.
num2 – Stores the second number.
result – Stores the calculated answer.

Important Conditions

switch(operator) – Checks which arithmetic operation the user selects.
if (num2 == 0) – Checks whether the second number is zero before division.
default – Displays an error message if the user enters an invalid operator.
Project Goal

To understand how to create a basic calculator using the switch statement in C. 
This program helped me practice user input, arithmetic operators, conditional statements, and decision-making logic. 
It also helped me understand how to handle division by zero and invalid operators.

